  "knowledgePattern": "Use logarithm laws to expose an arithmetic sum",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Simplify one general summand",
      "body": "<p>Apply the power law before trying to add the logarithms. Evaluate the fixed logarithm by writing its defining power equation.</p>",
      "recap": "The logarithm of a positive power multiplies the logarithm by that power; its base determines the sign.",
      "pitfall": "A base below one changes the sign of the logarithm of a number above one.",
      "pause": "Express the nth summand without a logarithm.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l06",
          "reminder": "The logarithm of a positive power multiplies the logarithm by that power; its base determines the sign."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Read the actual index range",
      "body": "<p>List the first and last simplified terms and count the indices inclusively.</p>",
      "recap": "An arithmetic block is summed from its first term, last term and number of terms.",
      "pitfall": "The index zero contributes a term even when its value is zero.",
      "pause": "Use the AP sum formula with the correct endpoints.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l08",
          "reminder": "An arithmetic block is summed from its first term, last term and number of terms."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> The logarithm power law gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>8</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>, the fixed logarithm is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, and the general summand is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> The index starts at 0 and ends at 20, so the first contribution is zero. Thus the sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>21</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>630</mn></mrow></math>. Answer A.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> As a check, the 21 terms are an AP with endpoints 0 and 60, whose sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>21</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>60</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>630</mn></mrow></math>.</p>"
